## Statement

Let $n\ge1$ and $a\in\mathbb R^n$. The linear map
$$
\ell_a:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R,\qquad \ell_a(y)=a^Ty,
$$
is measurable from the Euclidean Borel space $\mathbb R^n$ to the Borel space
$\mathbb R$.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** An integer $n\ge1$, a vector $a\in\mathbb R^n$, and the standard
Euclidean metrics and their Borel sigma-algebras on $\mathbb R^n$ and
$\mathbb R$.

[F1] Every linear map between finite-dimensional Euclidean spaces is bounded:
for some $K\ge0$, $\|Lh\|_2\le K\|h\|_2$ for every $h$.
[[lem-euclidean-linear-maps-have-matrices-and-are-bounded]].

[F2] A Lipschitz map between metric spaces is continuous.
[[thm-metric-regularity-hierarchy]].

[F3] A continuous map between topological spaces has Borel preimages of Borel
sets. [[thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel]].

[F4] A map between measurable spaces is measurable when the preimage of every
measurable target set is measurable in the source.
[[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]].

[F5] For $n\ge1$, $d_2$ is a metric on $\mathbb R^n$; its metric topology
determines the Borel sigma-algebra generated by open sets.
[[lem-metrics-on-rn]], [[def-metric-topology]], [[def-borel-sigma-algebra]].

## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 The map $\ell_a$ is linear. By [F1] and the Euclidean metric convention in [F5], choose $K\ge0$ such that $|\ell_a(h)|\le K\|h\|_2$ for every $h\in\mathbb R^n$. Thus for $y,z\in\mathbb R^n$, $|\ell_a(y)-\ell_a(z)|=|\ell_a(y-z)|\le K\|y-z\|_2,$ so $\ell_a$ is Lipschitz. [F1, F5, choose, algebra]

2.1 By [F2], $\ell_a$ is continuous for the Euclidean metric topologies from [F5]. By [F3], the preimage of every Borel subset of $\mathbb R$ is Borel in $\mathbb R^n$; [F4] therefore makes $\ell_a$ measurable. [F2, F3, F4, F5] ∎
